{"kind":"request","text":"G5: the hosted demo (plan 033, notes/plans/033-2026-10-18-grow-room-demo.md on main, order step 8, decisions 5 and 6, section 4 the simulator; grow note notes/2026-10-17-grow-operation-application-design.md sections 3, 6.3 and 7; docs/deploy.md \"Trusted application screens\"). Needs G1 to G3 (landed), G5a (in review at e0363323: its device-command owner is the simulator's role here; rebase onto G5a's landed head before the integration witness), the hosted MCP endpoint (live), and a deployment after review. Start now on the parts that do not depend on G5a's final form; ahead of P1 to P3 and S2b's runtime; S2a continues.\n\nScope. A room on the hosting deployment with the grow package installed, the simulator running as the sensors member and the device-command owner, and the /grow/ page with its link on the homepage.\n\n1. The package release. examples/grow packed as the demo package with dap pack and a publisher key, the signed packet committed in the head, and the implementation note printing the expected release and publisher exactly as dap pack prints them. The fixture package of examples/grow/check stays as the check's input. The operator sets APPLICATION_TRUST to that one row at deployment (docs/deploy.md); the head carries no trust setting and no key. The declared cadence and the fault story's period are in the package (plan 033 decision 6), in whole minutes.\n\n2. The simulator. A separate small Worker with its own Wrangler configuration beside the package (not the scope Worker), run by a cron trigger at the declared cadence, holding the sensors' agent key as its own secret. It speaks to the room only through the public member interface any agent uses: read the room's head, the device items and the open commands; compute the simulation's state as a function of the declared period, the clock and the commanded targets read from the journal; write the reading summaries, the report-device acts and G5a's owner answers for open commands, bounded per tick. No private state: a missed or failed tick leaves the next tick to catch up from the journal, and a redeploy changes nothing the room records. The fault story repeats on the declared period so a visitor at any hour finds the room somewhere in the story, with the incident count climbing. It is the only writer of reported state. No host change and no new host authority: the scope Worker learns nothing about the simulator. Name the per-tick bounds (acts, reads, bytes, time) and the cron's one-minute floor.\n\n3. The room and its runbook. A runbook in the head, in the form of examples/lending/runbook.md, for the operator to run after the deployment: claim the room, install the package by its immutable Git URI and expected release, enrol the sensors as an agent member with the operator as controller (dap join with the agent-key link), invite the operator and the head grower, set the simulator's secret and cron, and verify the first ticks. Builder's local runs and dry runs create no Cloudflare resource; the operator makes the deployment and runs the runbook after exact-head approval.\n\n4. The page. homepage/src/grow/index.html in the form of the counter and library pages, added to homepage/build.mjs, with a Grow destination card on index.html (homepage/test/home.test.mjs: five destinations) and the deploy doc's homepage paragraph updated. Content: the story in a few lines, a link that opens the live room in the browser room page, and how to connect an AI app to the hosted endpoint and take the acts (docs/mcp.md \"The hosted endpoint\"). No live grid on the page (G6). Visitor seats: say in the implementation note whether the existing page join flow of the Room destination can issue a bounded tender seat without a host change. If it can, offer tender seats that way, with a cited count; if not, the page says how to ask for a tender invitation, and the gap is listed. The operator's seat and the sensors' key are never offered.\n\nWitnesses, named in the review request: the packet verifies against its printed release and publisher; the simulator's tick on a local scope writes the three kinds of entry and answers an open command through G5a's owner step, with a second tick after a dropped tick catching up; a tick refused by the room (bounds, closed command, stale target) writes nothing else; the five built pages keep the homepage test's invariants; the runbook's steps each have a read that proves them. The hosted-deployment check (room created, install admitted, first ticks observed, the page's link opens the room) is the operator's, run after the deployment and recorded in the implementation note as a deployment receipt, not as a builder claim.\n\nDone when: a visitor opens the room from the homepage, a person in their AI app takes the operator's act during the fault, and the simulator keeps running between visits; gate passed once; checker approval at the exact head; the deployment and runbook by the operator after that approval. Section 5's exit test runs after the demo-room archive slice.","body":{"conditions":"G5: the demo room runs on the hosting deployment from the reviewed head with the trust row set; a visitor opens the room from the homepage; a person in their AI app takes the operator's act during the fault; the simulator keeps running between visits; witnesses named in the review request; gate passed once; checker approval at the exact head; deployment and runbook by the operator after approval","target":"inherit","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}